A||N||19||KES||Advice Pre purchase inspection (long)||||5||
Z||000000||KES||07-29-2000||09:49 PM||||I have been a vette enthusiast for some time. Always admired the Fiero but admittedly am devoid of knowledge about them. I have an opprotunity to aquire an 87 GT V-6 Auto cheap - may include a sapre engine with 115K on it.  The car has @215K on it. I ask humbly for feedback because though I'd love to rebuild the car - I don't as yet know how available parts are.  I nkow the engine will require rebuild, but would love to get it passing sticker for now. What I saw/heard.  Rt side mirrow must've got caught - was pulled back some and skin was slight torn under mirror - metal under must be pulled some.  metal door moulding on that side needs to be replaced.  front manifold must be cracked. cradle for engine has rust (can these be purchased?) area around one of passenger floor plugs is rusted out - rest floor looks fine. saw some rust flakes were the front cowl meets hood under cowl edge.  MArron and grey - some fading of paint in spots.  rust under master cylinder from fluid, rt rear quater glass cracked (can these be obtained still). rock chip in windshield and marks on side door glass. <BR>Technically doesn't looks pretty - but to restore need parts and not sure if available. admit my ignorance and appreciate your patience.  This is a 2nd owner car - saw all his receipts from purchase on.  seems put @2700 into it for front end and engine parts over last year.  Once again I appologize just didn't want to lose it if its worth saving for posterity. Any feedback, advice, things to look for etc is greatly appreciated - if it were a vette I'd nkow what to look at - But it isn't. These things have always caught my eye and it would be interesting to smoke a ZR1 in Bowling Green next year &lt;G&gt;<P>------------------<BR>Kevin Silva<BR>1993 ZR1 #307-Competition Yellow/Black<BR>1999 Ram 3500 4x4 QC-TurboDiesel<BR>NCM - #4738 <P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||olympic||07-30-2000||03:24 AM||||  We'll, from your description I think I would avoid this particular car.  Worn out parts can be replaced but rust is a different  problem all together.  That rust at the base of the windshield, on the cradle and around the floor plug are real warning signs.  This car must have been driven in a winter climate where they used road salt.  Also, at 200+k miles you'll need to worry about the tranny as well.<P>   87 GT's are not all that rare so I wouldn'y worry about "saving" this one.  Unless you can get it for a song and use it for parts I'd say keep looking.  There's plenty of well maintained low mileage GT's out there for a reasonable price.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-30-2000||07:17 AM||||I agree with olympic. Rust is a big concern. If you want to get a decent GT Just go to our "Mall" section there are a few for sale there. One is an 86 GT with only 52K miles on it. You can even post an ad in the Mall for what you are looking for (years, colors, options) and the state or area you live in and many members will help you locate the vehicle of choice. Ocassionally you can even find Fiero's with bigger engines for sale. One member just bought a Fiero with a Chevy 350 in it!<BR>By The Way welcome to the Forum!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||KES||07-30-2000||09:05 AM||||I thank you for your advice and warm welcome. <BR>orief how do i get to the "mall" - haven't found my way there yet. I was hesitant to make my first post such a doosey (sp)- normally prefer to lurk and learn vs being disrespecfull. knowing this forum exists makes me much less apprehensive.  I did neglect to mention that the price they ask is $1500. THat was why I was so interested - Figured I could rebuild the spare engine and do a swap at which time the tranny could receive an overhaul. the car is in Mass.(salt)- didn't know if metal sub pieces were replaceable/available.  Did figure out last night what I saw under the cowl was from someone being sloppy with brake fluid.  It also has the cutest holley ram air scoop on the quarter air intake :-) must be worth 15 HP. &lt;G&gt; Thanx again for your input. <BR>------------------<BR>Kevin Silva<BR>1993 ZR1 #307-Competition Yellow/Black<BR>1999 Ram 3500 4x4 QC-TurboDiesel<BR>NCM - #4738 <P>[This message has been edited by KES (edited 07-30-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by KES (edited 07-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||07-30-2000||01:44 PM||||I'd be very hesitant about getting $1500 into a car that I knew from the start would require a lot of work. I'd rather spend the extra $500-1000 to get a solid car with no rust and cosmetically good. To go to the mall, click on the button at the bottom of the page, scroll to Mall and hit go. That easy.<P>As an example, I know of an 85 GT with fresh motor, redone suspension, fresh michelin tires, and reupholstered seats for $1900 here in Oklahoma City. The deals are out on good solid cars. You will pay more initially then you would for a basket case, but you pay a lot less in the end. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||KES||07-30-2000||02:07 PM||||Thanx Stimpy, found the mall on my own finally - Duh - A mind is a terrible thing to waste &lt;G&gt; appreciate the feedback. Maybe patience is in order here.  Guess I got Fiero fever. Having not looked at others makes it dificult to guage the true condition of this one. thanx again<P>------------------<BR>Kevin Silva<BR>1993 ZR1 #307-Competition Yellow/Black<BR>1999 Ram 3500 4x4 QC-TurboDiesel<BR>NCM - #4738 <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||webbee||07-30-2000||02:19 PM||||Hi and welcome to the forum!<P>I always look at 3 cars of the type I'm going to buy before I get serious. After seeing a bunch the REALLY good deal stands right out. Every time I violate this rule it seems to bite me back. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000007||KES||07-30-2000||02:46 PM||||webbee - very sage advice. Whenever I violate a similar rule its always under the pretence "and if I don't jump on this now - I'll never have this opportunity again" &lt;G&gt; Fortunately, I haven't always been burnt, but the ratio good use improvement.  Looked a white GT this AM the guy wanted over 4K for and although there wasnt a spot on the floor panel - the car was worse of than the maroon one in some aspects<P>------------------<BR>Kevin Silva<BR>1993 ZR1 #307-Competition Yellow/Black<BR>1999 Ram 3500 4x4 QC-TurboDiesel<BR>NCM - #4738 <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||07-30-2000||04:12 PM||||Ditto to the previous advice. A year ago, I bought an 87GT for 2300. It was a two owner car, and the guy I bought it from owned it for 10 years (title revealed it). I live in Arizona, so rust doesn't even enter the equation, the quarter windows were cracked, it had a 5spd, it had just turned 100,000, and the seat was ripped. It was basically a solid car, and that was a year ago when they were probably valued a bit higher. Don't settle for a piece of crap when there are so many good ones out there. Now vettes are a different story. Unless you have $10,000 to spend, you must purchase a piece of sh!t.||||reg||1
Z||000009||My7Fieros||07-30-2000||04:30 PM||||I agree w/ everyone here. As much as I hate to have to part out a Fiero, (Ive saved some that some said were gonners)one with all that rust and that many miles for that price is really not a good deal in the least. If the frame is rusted bad enough, you might as well forget it. Im sure that car will make a great donor though.<BR>Judging by the way you collect Vettes, Im gonna take a shot in the dark and say that youve probobly got enough money to buy a nice, low milage fastback GT (if thats the body style you prefer)...its not too hard to find one for a great price, especially here!<BR>Anyway, keep your eyes open, one will come by thats just right for you...and welcome to the Forum!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||KES||07-30-2000||04:42 PM||||My7 - your shot in the dark was a bit off (unfortunately for me) but I do understand and appreciate your intent. Spending a few $ more now will save many $ later is what I am hearing in general. FWIW - The vette I own today represents the culmination of years of buy one fix it sell at a profit and on and on.  Can't say I haven't been fortunate. But can't say my last name is Rockafeller(sp) either.  This is beggining to seem like a very great place I've found here. <P>------------------<BR>Kevin Silva<BR>1993 ZR1 #307-Competition Yellow/Black<BR>1999 Ram 3500 4x4 QC-TurboDiesel<BR>NCM - #4738 <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||07-30-2000||08:22 PM||||I'd say that if you are in the market for a $4000 Fiero, you should be able to find something very nice. Look at fierodave's GT that he is selling in the mall... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/001105.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/001105.html</A> <BR>This is a hell of a deal from a guy who by all accounts is a stand-up guy with an impeccable reputation for turning out very thorough restorations on low mile cars. Makes me wish I was in the market for another Fiero.<BR> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||KES||07-30-2000||09:17 PM||||$4,000 Yikes (LOL) not at the moment for sure. But I'm also sure your probably right about the quality I could get for that.  I went throught the mall and saw Dave's car. If I am to begin looking for THE car at more $$ I think I want to stay 87 or 88 GT. I did write to a couple of the advertisers. You folks helping imensley (sp) both in educating me and feeding the "gotta have a fiero" fire. I'll sleep on this (told him I'd call tomorrow evening. Told the mrs I'm sure I can squeeze it through the double door in the basement to work on so she doesn't even lose a space in the garage &lt;VBG&gt; But suppose I don't need a planter in the basement either. that holley air scoop just looks so cool though &lt;G&gt; ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||07-30-2000||09:55 PM||||kes, if you're able to pull engines and rebuild/repair them i envy you. unless you've got to have a gt style fiero you might consider a 4 cly. coupe, making sure it is structuraly sound and putting in the engine you want. i guess that may not be the idea if you're into original restoration, but just a thought. i know here 4 cly. are easy to get. i just got one for $125 that as far as i can tell is very sound ...except for the engine (i saw this post as i was about to post about it).<P>and hey, if you're on the corvette forum too say something nice about fieros. they're red headed stepchildren there.||||reg||1
